<span class="ltx_personname">Xuying Meng
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Institute of Computing Technology, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Beijing, China
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Jing Li
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Harbin Institute of Technology, Shenzhen, China
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Aixin Sun
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Nanyang Technological University, Singapore
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Yequan Wang
</span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce EgoMem, the first lifelong memory agent tailored for full-duplex models that process real-time omnimodal streams. EgoMem enables real-time models to recognize multiple users directly from raw audiovisual streams, to provide personalized response, and to maintain long-term knowledge of users’ facts, preferences, and social relationships extracted from audiovisual history. EgoMem operates with three asynchronous processes: (i) a <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">retrieval</span> process that dynamically identifies user via face and voice, and gathers relevant context from a long-term memory; (ii) an <span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">omnimodal dialog</span> process that generates personalized audio responses based on the retrieved context; and (iii) a <span id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">memory management</span> process that automatically detects dialog boundaries from omnimodal streams, and extracts necessary information to update the long-term memory. Unlike existing memory agents for LLMs, EgoMem relies entirely on raw audiovisual streams, making it especially suitable for lifelong, real-time, and embodied scenarios. Experimental results demonstrate that EgoMem’s retrieval and memory management modules achieve over 95% accuracy on the test set. When integrated with a fine-tuned RoboEgo omnimodal chatbot, the system achieves fact-consistency scores above 87% in real-time personalized dialogs, establishing a strong baseline for future research.</p>
  
</div><span id="footnotex1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnotetext"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">footnotetext: </span>Indicates equal contribution.</span></span></span><span id="footnotex2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnotetext"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">footnotetext: </span>Corresponding author.</span></span></span>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A wide range of AI applications involve real-time lifelong omnimodal streams. A notable example is a robot deployed in homes and public spaces <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>. In this scenario, the models are required not only to follow instructions swiftly, but also to recognize users, remember their histories, understand social relationships, and deliver personalized services. Technically, the crucial capabilities to meet these requirements are summarized in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>]</cite>, including <span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">omnimodality</span>, <span id="S1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">real-time responsiveness</span>, and <span id="S1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">humanoid cognition</span>. Regarding <span id="S1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">omnimodality</span>, with the rapid progress of foundation models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>, <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>, substantial efforts have been made in incorporating audio <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">57</a>]</cite>, visual <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>, and action signals <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>, <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>, <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite> for joint understanding. For <span id="S1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">real-time responsiveness</span>, there have been solutions to achieve full duplexity, either based on time-division multiplexing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>, <a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">66</a>]</cite>, or on native duplex <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">62</a>]</cite> schemes. Yet, <span id="S1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">humanoid cognition</span> remains an underexplored capability for current omnimodal, full-duplex systems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">64</a>, <a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">63</a>]</cite>. In this work, we consider implementing the lifelong memory capability as a critical first step towards <span id="S1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">humanoid cognition</span>, because memory systems form the foundation of both human and artificial general intelligence <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>. We focus on real-time personalized dialog as a major task to validate lifelong omnimodal memory designs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In literature, two primary approaches have been explored to equip textual large language models (LLMs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite> with long-term memory: extended context windows <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>, <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>, <a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">55</a>]</cite> and memory agents <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">68</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">60</a>, <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>. However, neither method transfers well to <span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">lifelong omnimodal</span> scenarios. On one hand, extended context windows can retain long sequences of feature representation, encoding full omnimodal information <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>. Yet, in lifelong settings, the length of audio and visual streams grows without bound, making even million-token contexts insufficient <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite>, and eventually leading to the loss of information (<span id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">e.g.,</span> user identity). On the other hand, memory agent methods (Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ EgoMem: Lifelong Memory Agent for Full-duplex Omnimodal Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> (a)) are well-suited for lifelong operation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>, <a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>, <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>, but typically rely on several strong assumptions: user identities are explicitly known, dialog sessions have clear boundaries, and all inputs are textual (Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ EgoMem: Lifelong Memory Agent for Full-duplex Omnimodal Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> (b)). Unfortunately, these assumptions do not hold in full-duplex <span id="S1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">omnimodal</span> applications <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">65</a>, <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>, <a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">63</a>]</cite>. In these applications, the user identities are implicitly encoded in audiovisual streams, and the full-duplex nature means there are no well-defined boundaries for dialog turns or user sessions (Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ EgoMem: Lifelong Memory Agent for Full-duplex Omnimodal Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> (c)). Furthermore, existing memory agents generally overlook the multiuser social relation graph <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, an important element for humanoid cognition.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We showcase the role of an omnimodal lifelong memory agent in personalized dialog as follows. (1) When a user <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">Emily</span> shows up, there is a polling process that detects the user identity as <span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">Emily</span> directly from the audiovisual stream (<span id="S1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">e.g.,</span> camera and microphone inputs); (2) The profile of <span id="S1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">Emily</span> is encoded and put into the dialog context of an omnimodal chatbot; (3) When <span id="S1.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">Emily</span> asks <span id="S1.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">“does any of my colleagues love tennis?”</span>, a query regarding the relation <span id="S1.p3.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">“colleague”</span> and keyword <span id="S1.p3.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">“tennis”</span> is generated by the chatbot, activating a textual retrieval to the knowledge base containing Emily’s social relation graph, which returns a dialog record of <span id="S1.p3.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">“John, colleague, 2024-05-13, user discussed a tennis game he played 2 days ago”</span>. This record is further encoded as dialog context; (4) The chatbot answers <span id="S1.p3.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">“Yes, Emily, your colleague John loves tennis”</span> based on the available context; (5) An independent process extracts user facts: <span id="S1.p3.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">“Emily shows interest in tennis”</span>, and dialog record <span id="S1.p3.1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">“2024-05-14, user asked if any of her colleagues loves tennis.”</span>, from the raw audiovisual stream for the recent dialog, and updates Emily’s profile with these contents for future use; (6) When <span id="S1.p3.1.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">Emily</span> shows up on another day, the model is able to greet with: <span id="S1.p3.1.14" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">“Hi Emily, did you talk to John about tennis?”</span>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.11914v1/paradigm.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:640/297;" width="640" height="297" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span><span id="S1.F1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Different dialog dynamics for textual memory agents and full-duplex omnimodal memory agent (ours).</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Targeting on similar scenarios, we propose EgoMem, the first lifelong memory system tailored for omnimodal scenarios and designed to facilitate full-duplex personalized dialog. EgoMem operates through three asynchronous processes. First, a <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Retrieval Process</span> is responsible for real-time polling recognition of users, implemented with an audiovisual retrieval mechanism. It also contains a content-driven text retrieval module to gather related memory. This process facilitates efficient integration of both user-specific and RAG-style <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite> information into the dialog flow. Second, an <span id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Omnimodal Dialog Process</span> uses a fine-tuned dialog model to deliver full-duplex, personalized responses in real time, grounded in the retrieved context. Third, a <span id="S1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Memory Management Process</span> handles dialog boundary detection, information extraction, and memory updates by processing real-time raw audiovisual streams. This allows the system to continuously collect meaningful information at low cost and maintain an up-to-date memory over time.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Although the EgoMem methodology can be applied to any omnimodal backbone, we integrate it to RoboEgo <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">63</a>]</cite>, a <span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">native</span> full-duplex model that is best aligned with our target scenarios while achieving comparable dialog performances to state-of-the-art systems on general real-time conversation tasks. We extend RoboEgo with the full suite of EgoMem agent, and train both the dialog model and necessary sub-modules to deliver real-time, lifelong, and personalized responses to arbitrary users. We conduct automated evaluations across the audio, textual, and visual retrieval modules, the memory management module, and the system’s personalization abilities, considering either single-user profile (Level-1) and multi-user social graph (Level-2) as reference contexts. Evaluation results show that these modules exhibit high accuracy and robustness, and that the incorporation of EgoMem enhances personalization without compromising RoboEgo’s original dialog capabilities.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Our contributions are as follows:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Framework — We propose EgoMem, a lifelong memory agent for full-duplex, omnimodal interaction, which to the best of our knowledge is the first of its kind.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Implementation — We provide a concrete implementation of EgoMem based on the RoboEgo backbone, including detailed module designs, data construction pipelines, and training configurations.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Evaluation — We demonstrate that EgoMem achieves robust performance on personalization tasks in lifelong omnimodal scenarios, establishing a solid baseline for future research.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Task Definition and Preliminaries</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Task Description</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">EgoMem is designed to facilitate full-duplex, personalized chat for lifelong-deployed omnimodal models. As a first step, we focus on the case where there is only one active speaker at a time, leaving the more complex cocktail party problem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite> for future work. In this setting, in each time step <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, the main dialog model <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> takes as input (listening) audio <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, video <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, and optionally textual input <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, together with the user profile <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and reference information <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
              
              
              
            The time step <math id="footnote1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> can be a time index corresponding to real time. For example, RoboEgo <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">63</a>]</cite> runs for 12.5 steps to generate 1 second of speech.</span></span></span> It generates a personalized response <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{t}=F_{\theta}(a_{t},v_{t},p_{t},c_{t})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{t}=F_{\theta}(a_{t},v_{t},p_{t},c_{t}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> are commonly referred to as the <span id="S2.SS1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">context</span> or <span id="S2.SS1.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">short-term memory</span> in current literature, typically delivered to <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> as part of its input sequence and Transformer KV-cache <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">EgoMem manages an external memory unit <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, with three core functions: <span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">retrieval</span>, <span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">writing</span>, and <span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">updating</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Retrieval.</span> The retrieval function provides <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> to the dialog model by searching related information in <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> based on current dialog content:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t},c_{t}=\text{EgoMem.retr(}a_{t},v_{t},M\text{)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif">EgoMem.retr(</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif">)</mtext></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t},c_{t}=\text{EgoMem.retr(}a_{t},v_{t},M\text{)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In traditional RAG-based textual memory systems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>, <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is already determined before the dialog session begins, while the retrieval for <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is activated after the user’s each instruction, before the model’s final response. The retrieval is either conducted by an external module, or by the LLM fine-tuned to perform tool-calling. In lifelong omnimodal scenarios, a key distinction is that both user identities and dialog boundaries are arbitrary. The memory agent should be able to detect user identities and session boundaries from raw audiovisual streams by itself. In Section <a href="#S3" title="3 EgoMem ‣ EgoMem: Lifelong Memory Agent for Full-duplex Omnimodal Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we provide a complete set of algorithms for this function in EgoMem based on different forms of <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Writing.</span> The writing function extracts important events from lifelong multimodal streams and stores them in the memory unit <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Episode" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Episode</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\text{EgoMem.extract(}a_{0\sim t},v_{0\sim t},l_{0\sim t}\text{)}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif">EgoMem.extract(</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>∼</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>∼</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>∼</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif">)</mtext></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\text{EgoMem.extract(}a_{0\sim t},v_{0\sim t},l_{0\sim t}\text{)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle M</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leftarrow\text{EgoMem.write(}M,Episode\text{)}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif">EgoMem.write(</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif">)</mtext></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leftarrow\text{EgoMem.write(}M,Episode\text{)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p6.2" class="ltx_p">In EgoMem, memory writing is asynchronous to the main dialog, executed through independent processes. Unlike traditional memory agents, EgoMem takes as input the raw multimodal stream fragments in the dialog history (“episodic memory”), and extracts textual descriptions for the events, user persona, and other useful information.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Updating.</span> Similar to traditional memory agents, EgoMem periodically performs online or offline memory consolidation: it integrates existing memory into new, consolidated representations, and solves potential conflicts:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S2.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\leftarrow\text{EgoMem.update(}M\text{)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif">EgoMem.update(</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif">)</mtext></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\leftarrow\text{EgoMem.update(}M\text{)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Preliminaries</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we introduce the preliminaries for our implementation of EgoMem used in this paper. Nevertheless, EgoMem’s framework and methodology can be applied to arbitrary full-duplex omnimodal dialog models <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> and to different stream organizations of <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> beyond our implementation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Model Choice.</span> We adopt RoboEgo <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">63</a>]</cite> as our primary dialog model, since it supports a <span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">native</span> full-duplex scheme at least in the audio modality. The <span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">native</span> scheme features theoretically lower response latency and better scalability <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>, <a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">62</a>]</cite>. Also, in casual conversation and general instruction-following tasks, RoboEgo’s response quality and user experiences are comparable to state-of-the-art live chat systems such as Qwen-2.5-Omni <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Omnimodal Stream Processing.</span> For audio signals <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, we use the Mimi tokenizer <span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
              
              
              
            <a href="https://huggingface.co/kyutai/mimi" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://huggingface.co/kyutai/mimi</a></span></span></span> to extract features at 12.5 frames per second. Each audio frame is represented by one semantic token and seven acoustic tokens. The audio input and output are divided into two channels, <span id="S2.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">listening</span> and <span id="S2.SS2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">speaking</span>, while textual monologue tokens are placed in an additional textual channel. Thus, the model consumes 17 tokens in each forward pass including 1 textual and 16 audio tokens from the previous time step. They are additively merged into the input embedding. The model then processes all the historical input embeddings with a 7B LLM backbone to generate the hidden state for the current time step. Following the RQ-Transformer architecture <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">61</a>, <a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">69</a>]</cite>, a lightweight <span id="S2.SS2.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">depth</span> Transformer (with 100M parameters) first generates a textual monologue token based on the current hidden state on the top layer, and then generates eight speaking tokens autoregressively. In lifelong deployment scenarios, this process runs continuously in real time, forming the main dialog stream, while visual signals <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> are encoded through a Visual Transformer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite> and added into the context in a time-division multiplex (TDM) manner, at fixed intervals of 2–4 seconds. We refer the readers to the related work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">63</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">62</a>]</cite> for detailed structural configurations.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>EgoMem</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The design of EgoMem can be divided to two levels: Level-1, supporting multi-user personalization with each user’s profile, and Level-2, facilitating personalized dialog grounded in social networks, as well as other references. Compared to Level-1, Level-2 is particularly suited for application scenarios where users are more interconnected, such as home robots. For each level, we first describe the overall memory system design, and then the implementation details of each sub-module based on the primary dialog flow outlined in Section <a href="#S2.SS2" title="2.2 Preliminaries ‣ 2 Task Definition and Preliminaries ‣ EgoMem: Lifelong Memory Agent for Full-duplex Omnimodal Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Level-1: Profile-only</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In EgoMem Level-1, the memory maintains detailed profile information for each of its multiple recognized users, without recording the social relationship graph among the users, or other reference material. Formally, Level-1 EgoMem sets <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{t}=\text{None}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mtext>None</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{t}=\text{None}</annotation></semantics></math> in equations <a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2.1 Task Description ‣ 2 Task Definition and Preliminaries ‣ EgoMem: Lifelong Memory Agent for Full-duplex Omnimodal Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and <a href="#S2.E2" title="In 2.1 Task Description ‣ 2 Task Definition and Preliminaries ‣ EgoMem: Lifelong Memory Agent for Full-duplex Omnimodal Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.11914v1/level_1.png" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:668/298;" width="668" height="298" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span><span id="S3.F2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">System illustration for EgoMem Level-1 (Profile-only)</span>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.1.1 </span>System Design</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The system design of Level-1 EgoMem is illustrated in Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3.1 Level-1: Profile-only ‣ 3 EgoMem ‣ EgoMem: Lifelong Memory Agent for Full-duplex Omnimodal Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Storage.</span>
EgoMem Level-1 stores the profile of each user in a key-value pair: the <span id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">key</span> is two-folds, with one visual embedding vector <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{f}^{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>f</mi><mi>u</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{f}^{u}</annotation></semantics></math> for face verification and one audio embedding vector <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{s}^{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>u</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{s}^{u}</annotation></semantics></math> for speaker verification; the <span id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">value</span> can be abstracted as a dictionary storing the users’ name, personal facts, summary of previous dialogs, and preferences.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Level-1 EgoMem is driven by the following three component processes running asynchronously:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Retrieval Process.</span>
Different from traditional memory agents, our retrieval process runs in a lifelong manner with fixed intervals of 2 seconds (“polling”), instead of being triggered by end of user’s instructions or tool-calling from the primary dialog model. This is a critical design enabling the dialog model to <span id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">actively start</span> talking to the user (e.g., “<span id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Greetings John!</span>”). In every 2 seconds, EgoMem.retr() extracts a chunk of audio and visual signals with length <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>, and extracts query vectors:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle v_{f}^{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>f</mi><mi>q</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle v_{f}^{q}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E6.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\text{visual\_encoder}(v_{(t-\tau):t})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mtext>visual_encoder</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\text{visual\_encoder}(v_{(t-\tau):t}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle v_{s}^{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>q</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle v_{s}^{q}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E7.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\text{speech\_encoder}(a_{(t-\tau):t})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mtext>speech_encoder</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\text{speech\_encoder}(a_{(t-\tau):t}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{f}^{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>f</mi><mi>q</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{f}^{q}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{s}^{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>q</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{s}^{q}</annotation></semantics></math> are used to perform face and speaker verification with each <span id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">user</span>’s <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{f}^{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>f</mi><mi>u</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{f}^{u}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{s}^{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>u</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{s}^{u}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively, based on distances and thresholds (Section <a href="#S3.SS1.SSS2" title="3.1.2 Sub-modules ‣ 3.1 Level-1: Profile-only ‣ 3 EgoMem ‣ EgoMem: Lifelong Memory Agent for Full-duplex Omnimodal Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1.2</span></a>). If a valid user is found, its profile is tokenized with a textual tokenizer and pushed to the textual channel of a special <span id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Level-1 MemChunk</span> field in the main dialog’s token stream. This special chunk occupies a maximum length of 512 time steps, with its 16 audio tokens always filled with <span id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">&lt;empty&gt;</span>. It is always attended by every forward pass of the primary dialog model (Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3.1 Level-1: Profile-only ‣ 3 EgoMem ‣ EgoMem: Lifelong Memory Agent for Full-duplex Omnimodal Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>). Note that <span id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Level-1 MemChunk</span> is fully managed by the retrieval process: its textual content is refreshed once the current recognized user differs from the previous one. Every time <span id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p5.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Level-1 MemChunk</span> is updated, the main dialog process receives a signal and runs one forward pass that simultaneously updates the KV cache for all the textual tokens in this chunk. As this forward pass is only triggered by detected user switch, it introduces ignorable overhead in inference.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Omnimodal Dialog Process.</span>
This is the main process running the RoboEgo chat service. <span id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Level-1 MemChunk</span> is attended by EgoMem in each step. If no user profile is returned by the retrieval process, <span id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Level-1 MemChunk</span>’s text channel is filled with <span id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p6.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">&lt;pad&gt;</span> tokens. We fine-tune RoboEgo with the corresponding streaming data format (Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Training Details ‣ EgoMem: Lifelong Memory Agent for Full-duplex Omnimodal Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) to generate personalized spoken responses based on the user’s profile information.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Memory Management Process.</span>
This is an independent process instantiating EgoMem’s <span id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p7.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">extract, write,</span> and <span id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p7.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">update</span> functions (eq. <a href="#S2.E3" title="In 2.1 Task Description ‣ 2 Task Definition and Preliminaries ‣ EgoMem: Lifelong Memory Agent for Full-duplex Omnimodal Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> - <a href="#S2.E5" title="In 2.1 Task Description ‣ 2 Task Definition and Preliminaries ‣ EgoMem: Lifelong Memory Agent for Full-duplex Omnimodal Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>), mimicking humanoid memory activities. With a fixed time interval (e.g., 10 minutes), extraction is conducted on the 17-way token stream from the main dialog process: the memory management process takes as input a 8192-step stream chunk, labels each time step by a sequence tagging model (namely <span id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p7.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Episodic Trigger</span>) to find the boundaries of existing dialog sessions between RoboEgo and each user, and gather the corresponding raw streams for each dialog session. Next, we leverage an external LLM as <span id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p7.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Memory Extractor</span>, which is prompted to extract events, user facts, and user preferences from the clipped raw streams. Afterwards, the memory management process calls the face and speaker retrieval functions to identify the user of this session clip. If the user is identified as new to the system, EgoMem establishes a new user profile, stores the face/speech embedding vectors as keys, and initializes the user’s profile based on the extracted memory contents. Otherwise, the user identity and the extracted memory contents are provided to a <span id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p7.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Memory Update Agent</span>, which is an external LLM prompted to figure out potential conflicts between the extracted memory contents and the user’s existing profile. The user’s profile is updated accordingly.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.1.2 </span>Sub-modules</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The implementation of all the sub-modules used in the Level-1 EgoMem agent is shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3.1 Level-1: Profile-only ‣ 3 EgoMem ‣ EgoMem: Lifelong Memory Agent for Full-duplex Omnimodal Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Face Verification.</span>
We leverage an open-sourced pipeline from DeepFace <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>]</cite> to extract faces from video frames. Specifically, we use Retinaface <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> as a face detection backend, and Facenet512 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite> as the visual encoder, resulting in 512-dimensional face features<span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
                
                
                
              The retrieval ends if no face is detected.</span></span></span> for <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{f}^{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>f</mi><mi>q</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{f}^{q}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{f}^{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>f</mi><mi>u</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{f}^{u}</annotation></semantics></math>. In the retrieval process, we first find the closest existing user <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> by the minimal cosine distance <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=1-cosine\_similarity(v_{f}^{q},v_{f}^{u})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>f</mi><mi>q</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>f</mi><mi>u</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=1-cosine\_similarity(v_{f}^{q},v_{f}^{u})</annotation></semantics></math> to the query vector <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{f}^{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>f</mi><mi>q</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{f}^{q}</annotation></semantics></math>, and then verify with a pre-tuned threshold <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{\delta}=0.3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{\delta}=0.3</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{current user}=\begin{cases}u,&amp;\text{if~}d&lt;\delta,\\
\text{new user},&amp;\text{else}.\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>current user</mtext><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>new user</mtext><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>else</mtext><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{current user}=\begin{cases}u,&amp;\text{if~}d&lt;\delta,\\
\text{new user},&amp;\text{else}.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Speaker Verification.</span>
We leverage a wavelm_large <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> model fine-tuned specifically for speaker verification <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> as our speech feature extractor, producing 256-dimensional <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{s}^{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>q</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{s}^{q}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{s}^{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>u</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{s}^{u}</annotation></semantics></math> vectors. While speaker verification also relies on cosine similarity, it is widely accepted that applying adaptive s-norm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> to the similarity scores yields significant performance improvements. We use the Chinese sub-part of seed-tts-eval <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> dataset as imposter cohort containing 2,000 candidate samples. We tune the verification threshold based on Equal Error Rate (EER), with minor adjustments on the threshold based on case studies from our integrated EgoMem system.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Episodic Trigger.</span>
The episodic trigger is used to find the boundaries of dialog sessions in which the user’s identity is consistent. It not only detects the start and end of dialog sessions, but also splits the sessions from different users. Specifically, the episodic trigger predicts tags for each time step in the audio stream (including the aligned listen and speak channels):</p>
<table id="S3.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Tag}_{0\sim t}=\text{episodic\_trigger}(a_{0\sim t},r_{0\sim t})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext>Tag</mtext><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>∼</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>episodic_trigger</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>∼</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>∼</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Tag}_{0\sim t}=\text{episodic\_trigger}(a_{0\sim t},r_{0\sim t}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Specifically, the episodic trigger is an RQ-Transformer-based <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> model which shares the input stream organization and model structure topology with RoboEgo (Section <a href="#S2.SS2" title="2.2 Preliminaries ‣ 2 Task Definition and Preliminaries ‣ EgoMem: Lifelong Memory Agent for Full-duplex Omnimodal Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>), despite being much smaller with 100M parameters. It consumes the 17 audio-text channels with a maximum time step of 8192 as input. Instead of generating dialog responses, it assigns a label to each time step with the following paradigm: {0: <span id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">no dialog</span>; 1: <span id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">start of a new user’s dialog session</span>; 2: <span id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">in-session step</span>; 3: <span id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">end of current user’s session</span>}. We modify the attention mask from a GPT-like <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> causal mask to a Bert-like <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> full mask, as the sequence labeling process is offline and chunk-wise. The training configurations of episodic trigger is detailed in Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Training Details ‣ EgoMem: Lifelong Memory Agent for Full-duplex Omnimodal Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. The evaluation results are presented in Section <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2 Episodic Trigger Evaluation ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ EgoMem: Lifelong Memory Agent for Full-duplex Omnimodal Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Memory Extractor.</span>
The memory extractor is implemented as the following pipeline:</p>
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The episodic trigger labels each time step. According to the labels, audio chunks starting with label “1”, ending with label “3”, and correctly filled with “2” are considered as the audio source for one user’s dialog session. The corresponding audio waveforms are clipped.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The clipped waveform in the listening channel goes through automatic speech recognition (ASR). Raw ASR results are fed into the memory extractor. The response texts from the monologue text channel of the dialog model are also provided as reference.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We leverage a DeepSeek-V3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite> API to extract meaningful contents to store in the memory. Specifically, we prompt the model to summarize the dialog content with short, precise sentences, generate sentences describing the facts about the user, and figure out a 90-dimensional personal trail <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>, <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite> for the user. The user name (or “unknown_user”) is stored in a separate field.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Memory Update Agent.</span>
This is a DeepSeek-V3 API prompted to solve profile conflicts and formalize the extracted contents from the memory extractor, making sure that they are suitable for updating the structured memory storage.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Main Dialog Model.</span>
We further fine-tune the RoboEgo model to equip it with attention to the field of <span id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p8.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Level-1 MemChunk</span>, and capabilities to generate personalized dialog responses accordingly. The fine-tuning configurations are provided in Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Training Details ‣ EgoMem: Lifelong Memory Agent for Full-duplex Omnimodal Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. Unlike in Level-2 (Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Level-2: Content-driven ‣ 3 EgoMem ‣ EgoMem: Lifelong Memory Agent for Full-duplex Omnimodal Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>), the model does not generate any extra controlling signals or queries to EgoMem.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Level-2: Content-driven</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Level-2 EgoMem, the memory not only stores profiles for each recognized user as in Level-1, but also maintains the social relationship graph among the users, as well as other potentially helpful materials. Formally, Level-2 EgoMem provides both <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> in equations <a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2.1 Task Description ‣ 2 Task Definition and Preliminaries ‣ EgoMem: Lifelong Memory Agent for Full-duplex Omnimodal Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and <a href="#S2.E2" title="In 2.1 Task Description ‣ 2 Task Definition and Preliminaries ‣ EgoMem: Lifelong Memory Agent for Full-duplex Omnimodal Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. The major difference to Level-1 is that we set up an extra <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Level-2 MemChunk</span>, whose content is dynamically refreshed according to the queries generated by the primary RoboEgo model based on the ongoing dialog content, instead of being driven by the external polling process (as for the <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Level-1 MemChunk</span>). In real-time dialog processes, the current use’s profile is cached in <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Level-1 MemChunk</span>, while <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Level-2 MemChunk</span> caches the result of the primary model’s <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">active</span> retrieval to the database containing the entire social relation graph and facts for all users, as well as other external knowledge base.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.11914v1/level_2.png" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:668/291;" width="668" height="291" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span><span id="S3.F3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">System illustration for EgoMem Level-2 (Profile-only)</span>. We focus on showing the differences in retrieval process and hide the details for other processes like memory management.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.1 </span>System Design</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The system design of Level-2 EgoMem is illustrated in Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.2 Level-2: Content-driven ‣ 3 EgoMem ‣ EgoMem: Lifelong Memory Agent for Full-duplex Omnimodal Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, focusing on showing the
differences in retrieval process.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Storage.</span> The only format difference from Level-1 is that for each user, we add a field containing a list of triplets representing the edges from the current user to other users in the full social relation graph. Optionally, any other useful information can be added to the storage for similar RAG processes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The same three processes as Section <a href="#S3.SS1.SSS1" title="3.1.1 System Design ‣ 3.1 Level-1: Profile-only ‣ 3 EgoMem ‣ EgoMem: Lifelong Memory Agent for Full-duplex Omnimodal Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1.1</span></a> supports Level-2 EgoMem. We specify differences for each of them as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Retrieval Process.</span>
The <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Level-1 MemChunk</span> maintains its function in the system, while it is entirely managed by the external polling retrieval process. Further, an <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Level-2 MemChunk</span> with a maximum length of 256 is added to the attention context of the main model, while it is driven by the main model itself based on the dialog content. For example, within a dialog session between a specific user A and the RoboEgo model, the content of <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Level-1 MemChunk</span> is kept the same, caching user A’s profile. In contrast, after each user instruction, RoboEgo can activate an independent <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">textual</span> retrieval process to the database containing the name and personal facts from all users that are <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">connected to A in social relation graph</span>, together with other useful materials. A textual query is generated by RoboEgo on its monologue channel according to the current dialog. The result of this textual retrieval process is tokenized and cached in <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Level-2 MemChunk</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Omnimodal Dialog Process.</span>
With Level-2 EgoMem, the RoboEgo model is able to generate personalized responses based on both <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Level-1 MemChunk</span> and <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Level-2 MemChunk</span>. It is also allowed to generate textual query words on the monologue channel in arbitrary time step, marked by special tokens, which revokes the textual retrieval process as mentioned above.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Memory Management Process.</span>
The memory management process is generally the same as Level-1 with the only difference being the <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Memory Extractor</span> is prompted to also extract new relation facts from the raw dialog contents (e.g., User A says he is the boyfriend of User B now), and the <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Memory Update Agent</span> is prompted to link the user to existing known users accordingly, updating the edges of the existing social graph.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.2 </span>Sub-modules</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Level-2 EgoMem, the <span id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Face Verification</span>, <span id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Speaker Verification</span>, and <span id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Episodic Trigger</span> are kept the same as in Level-1 EgoMem. The <span id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Memory Extractor</span> and <span id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Memory Update Agent</span> differ from Level-1 only in prompts. The vital difference come from the following sub-modules:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Main Dialog Model.</span> For Level-2 EgoMem, we fine-tune the RoboEgo dialog model to also generate query words for the active textual retrieval process. Specifically, we allow two groups of query words formatted as <span id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">&lt;retr&gt;:\n&lt;group1&gt;\n&lt;group2&gt;&lt;answer&gt;</span>, with <span id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">group1</span> being the “relation query” and <span id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">group2</span> being the “keyword query”. Each group is a sequence of query words separated by comma. When the final <span id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">&lt;answer&gt;</span> token is generated, EgoMem activates a textual retrieval process to update the <span id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Level-2 MemChunk</span>. To fine-tune RoboEgo for Level-2 EgoMem, for each dialog turn, both the (old <span id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Level-2 MemChunk</span>, main token stream, ground-truth query words) triplet and the (new <span id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Level-2 MemChunk</span>, main token stream + retrieval words, ground-truth response) triplet are used as training samples. More training details are specified in Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Training Details ‣ EgoMem: Lifelong Memory Agent for Full-duplex Omnimodal Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Textual Retrieval.</span> The textual retrieval system gathers the top-K relevant textual information according to the query words generated by RoboEgo, and updates the content of <span id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Level-2 MemChunk</span>. Specifically, for each user <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> connected to current user <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> in the social graph, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>’s name and relation with <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> are concatenated with each of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>’s memory items (facts, dialog history, etc.) to form one candidate document for retrieval. If the “relation query” group is not empty, we first match all relevant users’ documents via the BM25 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>]</cite> algorithm using only the relation queries; next, if the “keyword query” group is not empty, we concatenate all the keyword into one string, and re-rank the retrieved documents based on their vector distances to this keyword string. We leverage the BGE-small <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> model as the textual encoder. The top-K results are returned to the textual channel of <span id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Level-2 MemChunk</span>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.11914v1/data_stream.svg" id="S3.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:509/202;" width="509" height="202"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span><span id="S3.F4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Token stream structure and supervision mask for EgoMem training data</span>.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Training Details</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now detail how to fine-tune RoboEgo to generate personalized response with Level-1 and Level-2 EgoMem. We also train the episodic trigger to label the dialog boundary for memory extraction. Interestingly, the data collection for these three training tasks can be unified in a same framework using supervision masks.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Data Collection</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Transcript Collection.</span> We first collect textual transcripts that simulates the lifelong personalized scenario with both Level-1 and Level-2 EgoMem.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For Level-1 EgoMem,</span> we first use DeepSeek-V3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite> to synthesize 500 user profiles including name, dialog history, and a 90-dimensional persona. Next, we synthesize 10k dialogs between user and AI assistant based on open-source instruct-following datasets (e.g., Infinity-Instruct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">67</a>]</cite>, WizardLM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">58</a>]</cite>, and multimodal question answering datasets involving visual inputs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>]</cite>): the user instructions are retained, while responses are refined by DeepSeek-V3 (or Gemini-2.5-Pro <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite> for VQA) to be more helpful and personalized. Finally, we prompt DeepSeek-V3 to include more question styles in which users ask questions regarding their own dialog history and profiles. Each dialog typically contains 3–5 turns.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For Level-2 EgoMem,</span> we first synthesize 500 possible relations (e.g., “father”, “colleague”), construct relation graphs linking one main user with 3–5 socially connected users. We prompt DeepSeek-V3 to generate questions requiring relation-graph reasoning (e.g., “Does my mother like physical exercise?”) and mix these questions with general instructions, producing 5k dialogs. For each question, the model annotates the effective query words (including both relation query and keywords query, both can be empty) sufficient to retrieve supporting facts from the profiles of connected users. After generating the query words, the model should also provide the ground-truth personalized response for training.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">TTS and Augmentation.</span>
Audio dialogs are synthesized from the collected transcripts. Each user utterance is assigned a random human voice, and converted into speech with Fishaudio TTS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite>, while model responses are consistently generated with a single fixed voice. For the listening channel, we add diverse noise from sources like DNS Challenge <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite> and RNNoise<span id="footnote4" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">4</span>
              
              
              
            <a href="https://github.com/xiph/rnnoise" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/xiph/rnnoise</a></span></span></span>, as well as random speech clips. Following Moshi <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>, we also simulate microphone echo by mixing the speaking channel into the listening channel with probability 0.3, applying random gain (0-0.2x) and delay (0.1-0.5s).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Token Stream Organization.</span>
We temporally concatenate the waveform of different dialogs to form full audio streams containing multiple sessions. With a probability of 0.3, a later user instruction <span id="S4.SS1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">interrupts</span> an ongoing model response, simulating the most widely-considered full-duplex scenario. The concatenated waveform are tokenized with a Mimi tokenizer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>, formatted as described in Section <a href="#S2.SS2" title="2.2 Preliminaries ‣ 2 Task Definition and Preliminaries ‣ EgoMem: Lifelong Memory Agent for Full-duplex Omnimodal Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>, and truncated to a maximum length of 8192. For the textual monologue channel, we record the actual start point of each audio response, and position the tokenized textual response to start 2 steps earlier. We follow the <span id="S4.SS1.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">natural</span> monologue strategy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">62</a>]</cite> instead of applying word-level alignment between text and audio <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>. For each tokenized training sample with length 8192, positions 0-511 are reserved for the <span id="S4.SS1.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Level-1 MemChunk</span>, and positions 512-768 for the <span id="S4.SS1.p5.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Level-2 MemChunk</span>, with dialogs beginning after these reserved slots. Figure <a href="#S3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 3.2.2 Sub-modules ‣ 3.2 Level-2: Content-driven ‣ 3 EgoMem ‣ EgoMem: Lifelong Memory Agent for Full-duplex Omnimodal Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> (top left) illustrates our token stream organization.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Supervision Masks.</span>
We apply three different kinds of supervision masks on the token streams introduced above, supporting the following three tasks:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For Level-1 EgoMem,</span> for each of the <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> multi-turn dialogs in a token stream, we position the corresponding user’s profile in <span id="S4.SS1.p7.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Level-1 MemChunk</span>, and set the supervision mask to 1 for the textual and speak tokens only in the time span of the current dialog, and 0 for other time steps, producing <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> distinct training samples in total (Figure <a href="#S3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 3.2.2 Sub-modules ‣ 3.2 Level-2: Content-driven ‣ 3 EgoMem ‣ EgoMem: Lifelong Memory Agent for Full-duplex Omnimodal Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, top right).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For Level-2 EgoMem,</span> more fine-grained supervision mask is applied to each turn <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{i},i\in[0,T_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{i},i\in[0,T_{j})</annotation></semantics></math> for each dialog <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{j},j\in[0,N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{j},j\in[0,N)</annotation></semantics></math> in a token stream. Specifically, for query word generation, we maintain the content of <span id="S4.SS1.p8.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Level-1 MemChunk</span> and <span id="S4.SS1.p8.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Level-2 MemChunk</span> for the previous turn, position the ground-truth query words right before the textual response for current turn, and set supervision mask to 1 from the audio start point of the current turn until the end of query words span (Figure , bottom left). For personalized response generation, we go through the textual retrieval process with the ground-truth query words to achieve the supporting fact from connected users in the relation graph, filling them into <span id="S4.SS1.p8.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Level-2 MemChunk</span>. <span id="S4.SS1.p8.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Level-1 MemChunk</span> is filled according to user identity. With these contexts, we supervise on the textual and speaking tokens from the end of query words to the audio end point of dialog, including the full response utterance (Figure <a href="#S3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 3.2.2 Sub-modules ‣ 3.2 Level-2: Content-driven ‣ 3 EgoMem ‣ EgoMem: Lifelong Memory Agent for Full-duplex Omnimodal Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, bottom right). To summarize, for each turn, the query words and personalized response are supervised separately with different context, yielding <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2*\sum_{j=0}^{N}T_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.055em">∗</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2*\sum_{j=0}^{N}T_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> distinct training samples from each token stream.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For Episodic Trigger,</span> we label each time step in the token streams to locate the boundary of the dialog sessions in the listen and speak channels as described in Section <a href="#S3.SS1.SSS2" title="3.1.2 Sub-modules ‣ 3.1 Level-1: Profile-only ‣ 3 EgoMem ‣ EgoMem: Lifelong Memory Agent for Full-duplex Omnimodal Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Training Configurations</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For Level-1 EgoMem,</span> we fine-tune RoboEgo with a dataset containing 158K samples with different (<span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Level-1 MemChunk</span>, token stream context, supervision mask) combinations. We duplicate the dataset by using both the original and noise-augmented listening channel. Training starts from one of RoboEgo’s SFT checkpoints, running for 5 epochs with batch size 64 and a cosine learning rate decay from 1e-5 to 1.5e-6.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For Level-2 EgoMem,</span> RoboEgo is fine-tuned on 54K samples with valid <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Level-2 MemChunk</span> and the corresponding query words, combined with 50% of the Level-1 training dataset reformatted with empty <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Level-2 MemChunk</span> and query words. As with Level-1, the dataset is duplicated with clean and noisy listening channels. Training resumes from the same RoboEgo checkpoint as in Level-1, running for 1 epoch with batch size 64 and a cosine learning rate decay from 1e-5 to 1.5e-6.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For Episodic Trigger,</span> the sequence tagging model is initialized randomly. We train the model with 100K clean samples and 100K noised samples. The number of epoch is set to 45. We use a batch size of 64 and the learning rate decays from 1e-4 to 1e-6 following a cosine schedule.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Experiments</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We focus on the following three research questions: (1) Do our retrieval sub-modules correctly identify users and recall the relevant contents? (2) Does the episodic trigger detect the correct boundaries of omnimodal dialogs? (3) Does the fine-tuned RoboEgo model effectively leverage the Level-1 and Level-2 EgoMem to deliver lifelong personalized responses? We answer these questions with quantitative results on dedicated benchmarks. As the first work in lifelong memory agents for full-duplex omnimodal systems, we will also release part of our test set for personalized omnimodal dialog generation to benefit future research.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Retrieval Evaluation</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We present the benchmark and results we use to evaluate the retrieval capabilities for EgoMem. The retrieval results are summarized in Table <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 5.1 Retrieval Evaluation ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ EgoMem: Lifelong Memory Agent for Full-duplex Omnimodal Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Face verification, speaker recognition, and text retrieval results for EgoMem sub-modules.</figcaption>
<table id="S5.T1.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S5.T1.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T1.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt">Tasks</td>
